<div class="side-by-side">
  <h2>The Challenge</h2>

  <p>
    Call of Duty: Mobile was launching DMZ: Recon, an extraction mode meant to
    become the third pillar of the game alongside multiplayer and Battle Royale.
    Activision needed the launch to land with the existing community and beyond
    it, in front of players of competing mobile shooters who had never tried
    COD: Mobile.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="side-by-side">
  <p>
    <b>Showcase the mode.</b> Show DMZ: Recon as a deep, replayable extraction
    experience, distinct from anything else in COD: Mobile.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Reach new audiences.</b> Build awareness among competitor players and
    non-endemic creators.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Win share of voice.</b> Capture attention across the crowded mobile
    shooter space at launch.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>The Solution</h2>

<div class="side-by-side">
  <p class="big">Awareness first, then proof of depth.</p>
  <p>
    Neither event alone would have told the whole story. The Extract-A-Thon was
    an open, low-pressure front door for players of every skill level. The
    Invitational followed as proof of depth: high-stakes, strategic and
    competitive. I planned both as one campaign, sequencing the timeline,
    formats and talent so each event set up the next.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="side-by-side">
  <p class="big">Extract-A-Thon: 24 hours, one continuous story</p>
  <p>
    A 24-hour marathon with one creator trio per two-hour block, scheduled
    around each region’s time zone so the broadcast stayed fresh for players
    across North America, Europe, Africa, the Middle East, India and Southeast
    Asia. Each team was handed three challenges. Completing all three unlocked a
    3x loot multiplier, and anything left unfinished rolled over to the next
    team, giving viewers a reason to keep watching through every rotation.
  </p>
</div>

